  Proceeding with my own approaches. The [38;5;153mcodex[39m binary is on PATH, but the gate runs through the plugin, which isn't
  installed — so no independent second opinion this round.

  [1mApproaches[0m

  All three assume the four locked decisions (staged multi-user, implicit+override subscription, typed events, channel
  seam + per-notification read state). They differ in [1mwhere the notification logic lives in slice one[0m, which is the
  thing that determines how much gets rewritten when the server arrives.

  [1mA. Domain core + swappable adapters[0m [3m(recommended)[0m
  A plain-JS domain module — [38;5;153mevents[39m, [38;5;153msubscriptions[39m, [38;5;153mnotifications[39m — with no knowledge of storage or UI. It exposes an
  async repository interface; slice one binds it to a [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m adapter, slice two binds the same interface to HTTP.
  The UI reads through a small view API. Trade-off: you write an interface you have only one implementation of, which
  feels like overhead today. Wins because the domain logic — subscription precedence, fan-out, dedupe — is the part that
  must survive the server transition unchanged, and here it survives by construction. Complexity: moderate.

  [1mB. Stub server, real HTTP from day one[0m
  Run an actual local API (a small Node/Express service) and have the browser talk real [38;5;153mfetch[39m to it. Trade-off: you get
  the true async/network shape immediately — latency, errors, race conditions — with no fake-to-real gap later. But it
  front-loads server infrastructure you said you'd stage, and you'll build persistence twice if the eventual backend
  differs. Wins if you know the real backend is starting within weeks. Complexity: high.

  [1mC. Direct client implementation[0m
  Notification logic written straight against [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m in the UI layer; extract abstractions when the server shows
  up. Trade-off: fastest to a working bell icon. But subscription precedence and fan-out end up entangled with
  rendering, and "extract it later" is exactly the rewrite the staged choice was meant to avoid. Wins only if multi-user
  might never happen — which you've said it will. Complexity: trivial now, expensive later.

  I recommend [1mA[0m. It's the only one that honors "staged" literally: the expensive, correctness-sensitive logic gets
  written once, and swapping [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m for HTTP touches one file. [1mB[0m is the better choice if the backend is imminent —
  so tell me if it is, because that changes my recommendation.

  Does this framing look right, and is A the one to develop into the design?

